 floating-point operation, multiply–accumulate instruction, or transistor transition. This review develops a hierarchical framework for locating energy costs in neural computation while distinguishing three quantities that are frequently conflated: process-specific thermodynamic lower bounds, Landauer-normalized reference ratios, and measured or modelled operational energy. The analysis synthesizes results from stochastic thermodynamics, finite-time and quantum Landauer theory, thermodynamic control, mismatch cost, device physics, computer architecture, and full-stack AI serving. The resulting hierarchy separates logical entropy reduction, finite-time corrections, quantum and strong-coupling effects, control costs, distribution-dependent mismatch, architectural and data-movement costs, and facility overhead. The literature supports rigorous bounds for specified physical proces

